# ord-v1 — ordered-comparison policy for worked example S2
#
# Exercises the ord@1 certificate path end-to-end, and shows the composition
# story the backend needs to be useful: an accepted comparison atom is
# TERMINAL unless a policy rule consumes it. Two rules, in the layering the
# design calls for (plan §3.6):
#
#   * beats_recheck — STRICT. The bare ordered comparison over two reported
#     score cells, re-checked exactly by the registered ord@1 backend
#     (src/Lara/Strict/Ord.hs, lean/Lara/Ord.lean). The certificate names its
#     two consulted premise slots; there is no witness value, because a
#     comparison over ground literals has nothing to recompute.
#
#   * beats_baseline — DEFEASIBLE. The bridge rule. It consumes the strict
#     num_lt conclusion together with a binding atom naming which systems and
#     measurand those two numbers belong to, and concludes the comparative
#     claim. This is what makes "this number beats that number" a SUPPORTED
#     CLAIM rather than a free-floating ground fact.
#
# The split is the whole point. The arithmetic is strict and unarguable; the
# inference from "0.71 < 0.74" to "sys_new is better" is defeasible, because
# it depends on the binding being right and on the comparison being the
# relevant one. You cannot argue with the arithmetic; you argue with the
# measurements and with the bridge.
#
# lara-syntax@0.3 adds three policy-level declarations at the bottom of this
# file — a measurand polarity, a comparison scheme, and premise labels on the
# bridge — so that examples/S2/example.lara can author the pair of arguments
# above as one `comparison` block. None of the three reaches the checker Unit:
# they select and name what the elaborator generates, and the generated unit is
# byte-identical to the hand-written one they replaced.

policy ord-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/S2/ord-v1.policy.lara examples/S2/example.lara

sort Experiment, Cell, System, Measurand, Dataset

con accuracy : Measurand
con exp1 : Experiment
con imagenet_val : Dataset
con score_cell(System, Measurand, Dataset, Num) : Cell
con sys_base : System
con sys_new : System

pred better(System, System, Measurand, Dataset)
pred comparison_setup(System, System, Measurand, Dataset, Num, Num)
pred num_lt(Num, Num)
pred reports(Experiment, Cell)

# --- The strict leg: the certificate-checked ordered comparison -----------------------------
# Slot order is the premise list order, and the certificate cites it: (prem 0) is the baseline
# cell, (prem 1) the system cell. The conclusion num_lt(Bv, Sv) says baseline < system, i.e.
# the system scores higher on a measurand where higher is better.
#
# Both compared values trace to a consulted premise (design §2.4): a threshold or public
# baseline is not a bare goal constant, it enters as a leaf feeding a premise slot.

rule beats_recheck(S, B, Q, D, Exp, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, score_cell(B, Q, D, Bv)),
                 reports(Exp, score_cell(S, Q, D, Sv)) ]
  conclusion = num_lt(Bv, Sv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:ord-v1-theory-0) ]

# The empty theory the ord@1 certificates cite: the certified comparison is pure arithmetic, so
# the theory contributes no entries — the digest is selection identity only (the S1 precedent).
# Emptiness here is a property of THIS policy, which the .lara door pins; it is not something the
# backend enforces. What ord@1 enforces is that theory entries are UNCITABLE (design §2.2): a unit
# may declare entries and still be accepted, but no certificate can consult one.
theory sha256:ord-v1-theory-0 = []

# --- The bridge: from a bare comparison to a comparative claim ------------------------------
# The strict conclusion num_lt(Bv, Sv) carries no measurand identity — as a proposition it is
# the same atom whichever measurands produced the two numbers (design §2.1, D15). The binding
# premise is what supplies that identity, and it is defeasible evidence like any other.

# The two premises carry LABELS (lara-syntax@0.3, App. B.4). A label is a name
# for a premise slot, so an attack can address the binding premise as
# `a2.binding.leaf` instead of counting to `a2.1.leaf` — see examples/S4, which
# does exactly that. Labels never enter the Unit: `binding` and `1` resolve to
# the same premise slot, and this rule's compiled form is unchanged by their
# presence. They are also checked against this rule's question ids at
# declaration time, so a label can never be read two ways at an attack site.

rule beats_baseline(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = defeasible
  premises   = [ cmp:     num_lt(Bv, Sv),
                 binding: comparison_setup(S, B, Q, D, Sv, Bv) ]
  conclusion = better(S, B, Q, D)

# --- The @0.3 comparison surface ------------------------------------------------------------
# Domain knowledge the artifact cannot supply and the elaborator cannot infer: on accuracy, a
# larger number is the better result. Nothing about how a number is USED recovers this, which is
# why it is declared here rather than derived at a use site. It selects which scheme below the
# `comparison` form reads; it never reaches the Unit.

measurand accuracy : Num where higher-is-better

# The scheme is keyed by the PAIR (relation, polarity), not by relation alone, because direction
# lives in the rules: beats_recheck's conclusion num_lt(Bv, Sv) is written baseline-first, which
# is what makes it the higher-is-better reading of "strictly better". Binding the scheme to the
# pair is what makes a mismatch between the declared polarity and the rules' direction nameable;
# the elaborator's direction-of-goodness check (App. B.2) is what rejects it. Flipping this
# conclusion to num_lt(Sv, Bv) AND the bridge's cmp premise to match — an internally consistent
# edit that nothing else notices — is caught here rather than certifying the opposite claim.
#
# Rule names are policy-specific — examples/S3 spells the same two roles tie_recheck/no_worse —
# so this indirection is what lets one `comparison` form serve both policies.

comparison-scheme strictly-better higher-is-better
  recheck = beats_recheck
  bridge  = beats_baseline

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): the only strict rule is beats_recheck, whose conclusion
# pattern num_lt(Bv, Sv) heads no contrary pair, so no contrary side is strict-reachable and the
# policy passes the R12 check. That is a theorem, not a convenience: comparison goals are
# decidable against a shared ground truth, so a sound backend can never accept two conflicting
# members of the {num_lt, num_le} family over the same literals — mechanized as the
# intra-family exclusion lemmas in lean/Lara/Ord.lean (lt_excl_lt, lt_excl_le, lifted to
# ordModels_excl_of_lt). Declared contraries would add nothing and would trip R12.
